# netsurf: flex/grid containers end margin collapsing; stretched nested flex items keep their size; border-box min/max-height
# Generated 2026-09-30
#
# google.com's header, continued (after patch 180):
# - layout.c layout_next_margin_block(): a flex or grid container
#   establishes its own formatting context, so margins never collapse
#   THROUGH it (CSS 2.1 8.3.1). The walk went past an auto-height one and
#   took the NEXT block's top margin: google's footer (margin-top: -10px,
#   after the 808px #gb-main column) pulled <body> 10px above the page
#   top, the header's tabs under the toolbar; any page opening with a flex
#   or grid container was shifted by the following block's margin
#   (L3-grid and L4-flex: their first row moves from y 12 to y 0, where
#   Chromium has it).
# - layout_flex.c: a flex/grid container stretched on the cross axis kept
#   its content height after its relayout -- google's tab row stayed 17px
#   in the 48px header. The stretched size is its used size (9.4.11).
# - layout.c layout_apply_minmax_height() and layout_internal.h
#   (layout_find_dimensions): box-sizing: border-box applies to
#   min-height / max-height as it did to height and the widths; google's
#   Sign-in (border-box, min-height 40px, padding 10px) was 60px tall.
# Test: CSS-flex-header.html 6/6 (Chromium 6/6): spacer grows, last item
# as wide as its content, tabs stretched to the row, body at the top,
# calc() line-height, border-box min-height. Scorecard: google 63.0 ->
# 64.8, archwiki +3.2; bbc/rte unchanged against the pre-180 build on the
# same fresh references; guardian's nav bar now at Chromium's y (its
# visual score reads lower against a cookie-dialog reference).
